{"id": "SATM2R1E7AH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The function $f(x) = ax + 320$ models the number of pages remaining to be read in a book after $x$ days. After $6$ days, $200$ pages remain. After how many total days will the student have read exactly $280$ pages?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "14", "explanation": "First, find $a$ using the condition $f(6) = 200$: $6a + 320 = 200$, so $6a = -120$ and $a = -20$. The function is $f(x) = -20x + 320$. The book has $f(0) = 320$ pages total. The number of pages read after $x$ days is $320 - f(x) = 320 - (-20x + 320) = 20x$. Set $20x = 280$ to get $x = 14$. A common error is solving $f(x) = 280$ instead, which confuses pages remaining with pages read and gives $x = -2$. Another error is finding $a$ incorrectly by dividing $200$ by $6$, which ignores the initial $320$ pages."}
{"id": "SATM2R2VWJ2", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $g$ satisfies $g(3) = 17$ and $g(11) = 53$. What is the value of $g(0)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "3.5", "explanation": "First, find the slope: $m = \\frac{53 - 17}{11 - 3} = \\frac{36}{8} = \\frac{9}{2}$. Using point-slope form with $(3, 17)$: $g(x) - 17 = \\frac{9}{2}(x - 3)$, so $g(x) = \\frac{9}{2}x - \\frac{27}{2} + 17 = \\frac{9}{2}x - \\frac{27}{2} + \\frac{34}{2} = \\frac{9}{2}x + \\frac{7}{2}$. Therefore $g(0) = \\frac{7}{2} = 3.5$."}
{"id": "SATM2R917HI", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A linear function $f$ has $f(0) = 11$ and $f(1) = 17$. What is the value of $f(3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$23$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$29$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$33$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$35$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope is $\\dfrac{17 - 11}{1 - 0} = 6$, and the $y$-intercept is $11$, so $f(x) = 6x + 11$. Therefore $f(3) = 6(3) + 11 = 18 + 11 = 29$. The value $23$ equals $f(2) = 12 + 11$, resulting from evaluating at the wrong input. The value $33$ equals $11 \\times 3$, which incorrectly multiplies the $y$-intercept by $3$ instead of using the linear function. The value $35$ results from using an incorrect slope of $8$: $8(3) + 11 = 35$."}
{"id": "SATM2RICQZM", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -4x + 23$. What is the value of $g(-3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-35$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-11$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$11$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$35$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "Substituting $x = -3$: $g(-3) = -4(-3) + 23 = 12 + 23 = 35$. The value $11$ results from evaluating $-4(3) + 23 = -12 + 23$, treating the input as positive $3$ instead of $-3$, a sign error. The value $-11$ results from computing $-4(-3) - 23 = 12 - 23$, subtracting the constant instead of adding it, an arithmetic slip. The value $-35$ results from computing $-4(3) - 23 = -12 - 23$, combining both errors."}
{"id": "SATM2RK9H61", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -11x + 143$. For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "13", "explanation": "Setting $g(x) = 0$ gives $-11x + 143 = 0$. Subtracting $143$ from both sides yields $-11x = -143$. Dividing both sides by $-11$ gives $x = 13$."}
{"id": "SATM2RMUIEG", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 13x - 29$. What is the value of $f(4)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "23", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 4$ into $f(x) = 13x - 29$ gives $f(4) = 13(4) - 29 = 52 - 29 = 23$."}
{"id": "SATM2RQ0FPC", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(2) = 17$ and $f(8) = 47$. What is the rate of change of $f$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$10$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The rate of change of a linear function is the slope: $\\frac{f(8) - f(2)}{8 - 2} = \\frac{47 - 17}{6} = \\frac{30}{6} = 5$. The value $3$ results from computing $\\frac{47 - 17}{8 + 2} = \\frac{30}{10} = 3$, adding the $x$-values instead of subtracting (arithmetic slip in the denominator). The value $6$ comes from inverting the fraction and computing $\\frac{8 - 2}{47 - 17} = \\frac{6}{30} = \\frac{1}{5}$, but then misreading the result as $6$ (the denominator difference). The value $10$ results from using only the difference $47 - 17 = 30$ and dividing by $3$ (perhaps confusing $f(2) = 17$ as having $x = 3$) or from a partial calculation."}
{"id": "SATM2RQTN4I", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A gym charges a one-time registration fee of 37 dollars plus 19 dollars per month. Which function $f(m)$ gives the total cost, in dollars, after $m$ months?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$f(m) = 19m + 37$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$f(m) = 37m + 19$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$f(m) = 19m - 37$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$f(m) = 56m$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The monthly charge of 19 dollars is the rate of change (slope), and the one-time fee of 37 dollars is the initial value ($y$-intercept), giving $f(m) = 19m + 37$. The function $37m + 19$ swaps the rate and the initial fee. The function $19m - 37$ uses the wrong sign for the registration fee, subtracting it instead of adding. The function $56m$ incorrectly combines the rate and fee into a single coefficient, treating the entire amount as a monthly rate."}
{"id": "SATM2RR8J4S", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $p$ satisfies $p(2) = 13$ and $p(6) = 37$. What is the value of $p(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-11$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{13}{2}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$6$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{37 - 13}{6 - 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6$. Using the point $(2, 13)$: $p(x) = 6(x - 2) + 13 = 6x - 12 + 13 = 6x + 1$. Therefore $p(0) = 1$. The distractor $-11$ results from an arithmetic slip, subtracting $24$ from $13$ instead of computing $13 - 12 = 1$. The distractor $\\frac{13}{2}$ comes from dividing $p(2) = 13$ by $2$, incorrectly assuming $p(0) = \\frac{p(2)}{2}$. The distractor $6$ comes from misreading the question and reporting the slope rather than computing $p(0)$."}
{"id": "SATM2RU1ATJ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = ax + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $g(3) = 22$ and $g(7) = 46$, for what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 100$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "16", "explanation": "Find the slope: $a = \\frac{g(7) - g(3)}{7 - 3} = \\frac{46 - 22}{4} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6$. Substitute into $g(3) = 22$: $6(3) + b = 22$, so $18 + b = 22$ and $b = 4$. The function is $g(x) = 6x + 4$. Set $g(x) = 100$: $6x + 4 = 100$, so $6x = 96$ and $x = 16$. A common error is finding the slope correctly but computing $b$ from $g(7)$: $46 - 6(7) = 46 - 42 = 4$, which actually also yields $b = 4$ (consistent), but a sign slip such as $46 - 6(3) = 46 - 18 = 28$ (using the wrong point's $x$ with the other point's output) gives $b = 28$ and then $6x + 28 = 100$, yielding $x = 12$. Another error is solving $6x + 4 = 100$ as $6x = 104$ (adding $4$ instead of subtracting), giving $x = \\frac{104}{6} \\approx 17.3$."}
{"id": "SATM2S13P8W", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A car rental company charges a flat fee plus a per-mile rate. The total cost $C$, in dollars, for driving $d$ miles is given by the function $C(d) = 0.35d + 52$. What does the value $0.35$ represent in this context?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The total cost, in dollars, of renting the car for one mile"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The flat fee, in dollars, charged regardless of how many miles are driven"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The increase in total cost, in dollars, for each additional mile driven"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The number of miles included before additional charges apply"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is that $0.35$ represents the increase in total cost, in dollars, for each additional mile driven. In the linear function $C(d) = 0.35d + 52$, the coefficient of $d$ is the rate of change, meaning the cost increases by $\\$0.35$ for every additional mile. The choice describing a flat fee confuses the slope with the $y$-intercept; the flat fee is $\\$52$. The choice about the total cost for one mile incorrectly equates the per-mile rate with the total cost at $d = 1$, which would actually be $C(1) = 52.35$. The choice about miles included misinterprets the slope as a mileage threshold."}
{"id": "SATM2S1T64N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $h$ satisfies $h(2) = 19$ and $h(9) = -16$. For what value of $x$ does $h(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "29/5", "explanation": "The slope of $h$ is $\\frac{-16 - 19}{9 - 2} = \\frac{-35}{7} = -5$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 19)$: $h(x) = -5(x - 2) + 19 = -5x + 10 + 19 = -5x + 29$. Setting $h(x) = 0$: $-5x + 29 = 0$, so $5x = 29$, giving $x = \\frac{29}{5}$. Verification: $h\\left(\\frac{29}{5}\\right) = -5 \\cdot \\frac{29}{5} + 29 = -29 + 29 = 0$."}
{"id": "SATM2S3SFG7", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -3x + b$, where $b$ is a constant. If $g(7) = 19$, what is the value of $g(-4)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$28$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$40$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$52$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-52$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Since $g(7) = -3(7) + b = -21 + b = 19$, it follows that $b = 40$. So $g(x) = -3x + 40$. Then $g(-4) = -3(-4) + 40 = 12 + 40 = 52$. The value $28$ results from computing $-3(-4)$ as $-12$ instead of $12$, giving $-12 + 40 = 28$. The value $40$ comes from finding $b$ but not evaluating $g(-4)$. The value $-52$ results from a sign error in the final calculation."}
{"id": "SATM2S5WUOH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $g$ satisfies $g(2) = 17$ and $g(9) = -18$. What is the value of $g(0)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "27", "explanation": "The rate of change (slope) of $g$ is $\\frac{-18 - 17}{9 - 2} = \\frac{-35}{7} = -5$. Using the point $(2, 17)$: $g(x) = -5(x - 2) + 17 = -5x + 10 + 17 = -5x + 27$. Therefore $g(0) = -5(0) + 27 = 27$."}
{"id": "SATM2SDWY1J", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $h$ is defined by $h(x) = -5x + 22$. What is the value of $h(3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$37$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$15$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Substitute $x = 3$: $h(3) = -5(3) + 22 = -15 + 22 = 7$. The value $37$ comes from adding $15 + 22$ instead of $-15 + 22$. The value $-7$ reverses the subtraction to get $15 - 22$. The value $15$ is the intermediate product $5 \\cdot 3$ before applying the negative sign and adding $22$."}
{"id": "SATM2SFM9HK", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $h$ is defined by $h(x) = -13x + 247$. For what value of $x$ does $h(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "19", "explanation": "Setting $h(x) = 0$ gives $-13x + 247 = 0$. Subtracting $247$ from both sides yields $-13x = -247$. Dividing both sides by $-13$ gives $x = 19$."}
{"id": "SATM2SHO3FY", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(2) = 13$, $f(5) = 28$, and $f(8) = 43$. What is the value of $f(14)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$58$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$63$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$73$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$78$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{28 - 13}{5 - 2} = \\frac{15}{3} = 5$. Using the point $(2, 13)$: $f(x) = 5(x - 2) + 13 = 5x + 3$. Evaluating at $x = 14$ gives $f(14) = 5(14) + 3 = 73$. The value 58 results from computing $f(11)$ instead of $f(14)$ or adding one fewer interval. The value 63 comes from an arithmetic slip in the final calculation. The value 78 results from using an incorrect $y$-intercept of 8 instead of 3."}
{"id": "SATM2SJ79IS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -3x + 12$. If $f(a) = 0$, what is the value of $f(2a)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-24$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-12$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$0$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$12$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is $-12$. First, find $a$ by setting $f(a) = 0$: $-3a + 12 = 0$, so $a = 4$. Then $2a = 8$, and $f(8) = -3(8) + 12 = -24 + 12 = -12$. $-24$ results from computing $-3(8) = -24$ but forgetting to add $12$. $0$ incorrectly assumes that $f(2a) = 0$ because $f(a) = 0$. $12$ results from a sign error, computing $3(8) - 12 = 12$."}
{"id": "SATM2SPPTJT", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $g$ is defined such that $g(2) = 31$ and $g(9) = -18$. For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "45/7", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-18 - 31}{9 - 2} = \\frac{-49}{7} = -7$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 31)$: $g(x) = -7(x - 2) + 31 = -7x + 14 + 31 = -7x + 45$. Setting $g(x) = 0$: $7x = 45$, so $x = \\frac{45}{7}$."}
{"id": "SATM2SQVF4T", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A bakery models its daily production cost in dollars with the function $C(x) = 8x + 50$, where $x$ is the number of cakes produced. What does the number $8$ represent in this function?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The cost per cake produced"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The total number of cakes produced"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The fixed daily operating cost"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The total daily production cost"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is \"The cost per cake produced.\" In the function $C(x) = 8x + 50$, the coefficient of $x$ represents the rate of change, which is the additional cost for each cake produced. The constant $50$ represents the fixed cost, not the coefficient $8$. \"The fixed daily operating cost\" confuses the slope with the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM2SYP6H3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 8x - 23$. What is the value of $g(7)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-8$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$33$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$56$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$79$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 7$: $g(7) = 8(7) - 23 = 56 - 23 = 33$. The value $-8$ results from adding $8$ and $7$ instead of multiplying, then subtracting $23$: $8 + 7 - 23 = -8$. The value $56$ results from computing $8 \\times 7 = 56$ but stopping before subtracting $23$. The value $79$ results from adding $23$ instead of subtracting it: $56 + 23 = 79$."}
{"id": "SATM2SZOF6F", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(2) = 17$ and $f(9) = -18$. What is $f(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$27$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-27$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$22$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{-18 - 17}{9 - 2} = \\frac{-35}{7} = -5$. Using point-slope form with $(2, 17)$: $f(x) = -5(x - 2) + 17 = -5x + 10 + 17 = -5x + 27$. Therefore $f(0) = -5(0) + 27 = 27$. The value $7$ comes from computing the slope correctly as $-5$ and the adjustment $(-5)(0 - 2) = 10$, but subtracting instead of adding: $17 - 10 = 7$. The value $-27$ results from a sign error in computing the $y$-intercept, negating the result. The value $22$ comes from an arithmetic slip such as computing $-5(2) + 17 = -10 + 17 = 7$ and then incorrectly adjusting to find $f(0) = 17 + 5 = 22$ by adding one slope step in the wrong direction."}
{"id": "SATM2SZQ38Q", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "If $f(x) = 4x - 7$, what is the value of $f(3)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-16$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$0$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$19$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Substituting $3$ for $x$ gives $f(3) = 4(3) - 7 = 12 - 7 = 5$. The value $19$ results from adding $7$ instead of subtracting it, computing $12 + 7$. The value $-16$ comes from subtracting $7$ from $3$ before multiplying by $4$, computing $4(3 - 7) = 4(-4) = -16$. The value $0$ results from adding $4 + 3 - 7 = 0$ rather than multiplying $4$ by $3$ first."}
{"id": "SATM2T60XNO", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $f$ satisfies $f(3) = 22$ and $f(11) = 54$. What is the value of $f(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-10$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$10$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$34$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is $10$. First find the slope: $m = \\frac{54 - 22}{11 - 3} = \\frac{32}{8} = 4$. Using $f(3) = 22$: $4(3) + b = 22$, so $12 + b = 22$ and $b = 10$. Therefore $f(x) = 4x + 10$, and $f(0) = 10$. The value $4$ comes from finding the slope but reporting it as $f(0)$ instead of computing the $y$-intercept. The value $34$ results from an arithmetic error such as computing $f(0) = 22 + 4(3) = 34$ instead of $22 - 4(3) = 10$. The value $-10$ is a sign error on the correct $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM2T7PT14", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 13x - 47$. What is the value of $g(6)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-31$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$25$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$31$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$78$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 6$ into $g(x) = 13x - 47$ gives $g(6) = 13(6) - 47 = 78 - 47 = 31$. The value $-31$ results from a sign error, computing $47 - 78$ instead of $78 - 47$. The value $25$ comes from an arithmetic slip such as computing $13 \\times 6 = 72$ instead of $78$, giving $72 - 47 = 25$. The value $78$ results from computing only $13 \\times 6$ without subtracting $47$."}
{"id": "SATM2TALBJB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $g(x) = mx + 19$ passes through the point $(7, -16)$. What is the value of $g(3)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "4", "explanation": "Since $g(7) = -16$, substituting gives $7m + 19 = -16$. Solving: $7m = -35$, so $m = -5$. The function is $g(x) = -5x + 19$. Evaluating at $x = 3$: $g(3) = -5(3) + 19 = -15 + 19 = 4$."}
{"id": "SATM2TE7QZM", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(2) = 11$ and $f(6) = 31$. What is the value of $f(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$21$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{31 - 11}{6 - 2} = \\frac{20}{4} = 5$. Using $f(2) = 11$: $11 = 5(2) + b$, so $b = 1$, meaning $f(0) = 1$. The value $-1$ arises from a sign error when solving for $b$, such as computing $10 - 11 = -1$. The value $5$ reflects partial reasoning where the student identifies the slope but reports it as the answer instead of finding the $y$-intercept. The value $21$ comes from an equation-setup error, such as averaging $11$ and $31$."}
{"id": "SATM2TGZNCL", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A swimming pool is being drained at a constant rate. After 3 hours, the pool contains 4,700 gallons of water, and after 8 hours, it contains 2,200 gallons. What is the rate of change, in gallons per hour?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$500$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-600$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-500$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$600$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{2200 - 4700}{8 - 3} = \\frac{-2500}{5} = -500$ gallons per hour. The negative sign indicates the pool is losing water. The value $500$ drops the negative sign, failing to reflect that the quantity is decreasing. The value $-600$ results from dividing $-2500$ by an incorrect time difference. The value $600$ compounds both errors by using the wrong interval and ignoring the sign."}
{"id": "SATM2THVA36", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A candle is $29$ centimeters tall when it is first lit. After burning for $t$ hours, its height can be modeled by the linear function $h(t) = 29 - \\frac{7}{3}t$, where $h(t)$ is measured in centimeters. After how many hours will the candle be $8$ centimeters tall?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "9", "explanation": "Setting $h(t) = 8$: $29 - \\frac{7}{3}t = 8$. Subtracting $29$ from both sides gives $-\\frac{7}{3}t = -21$. Multiplying both sides by $-\\frac{3}{7}$ yields $t = (-21)\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right) = \\frac{63}{7} = 9$. The candle will be $8$ centimeters tall after $9$ hours."}
{"id": "SATM2TIM246", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = \\frac{7x - 19}{3}$. If $g(a) = 2a - 11$, what is the value of $a$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-14$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-8$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$8$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$14$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $-14$. Setting $g(a) = 2a - 11$ gives $\\frac{7a - 19}{3} = 2a - 11$. Multiplying both sides by $3$ yields $7a - 19 = 6a - 33$. Subtracting $6a$ from both sides gives $a - 19 = -33$, and adding $19$ to both sides gives $a = -14$. To verify: $g(-14) = \\frac{7(-14) - 19}{3} = \\frac{-117}{3} = -39$, and $2(-14) - 11 = -39$. The value $-8$ comes from an arithmetic slip in computing $-33 + 19$, getting $-8$ instead of $-14$ (as if computing $-19 + 11$). The value $8$ results from failing to distribute the $3$ to the $-11$ term, solving $7a - 19 = 6a - 11$ to get $a = 8$. The value $14$ results from a sign error, producing $a = 14$ instead of $a = -14$."}
{"id": "SATM2TUNNZ4", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A swimming pool is being drained at a constant rate. At $t = 0$ minutes, the pool contains 4,700 gallons of water. After 15 minutes, the pool contains 3,425 gallons. Which function $w$ models the amount of water, in gallons, remaining in the pool after $t$ minutes?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$w(t) = 3{,}425 - 85t$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$w(t) = 4{,}700 - 85t$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$w(t) = 4{,}700 - \\frac{t}{85}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$w(t) = 4{,}700 + 85t$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{3{,}425 - 4{,}700}{15} = \\frac{-1{,}275}{15} = -85$ gallons per minute. Starting at 4,700 gallons, the function is $w(t) = 4{,}700 - 85t$. The function $w(t) = 3{,}425 - 85t$ incorrectly uses the 15-minute reading as the initial value instead of the value at $t = 0$. The function $w(t) = 4{,}700 - \\frac{t}{85}$ inverts the rate, confusing gallons per minute with minutes per gallon. The function $w(t) = 4{,}700 + 85t$ applies a positive rate, which would model filling rather than draining."}
{"id": "SATM2U54UOY", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $f$ has a rate of change of $-4$. If $f(3) = 17$, what is $f(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-12$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$17$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$29$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "Since $f$ is linear with slope $-4$, we have $f(x) = -4x + b$. Substituting $f(3) = 17$ gives $-12 + b = 17$, so $b = 29$. Therefore $f(0) = 29$. The value $-12$ is the intermediate product $-4 \\cdot 3$ and represents stopping before completing the calculation. The value $5$ results from using a positive slope of $4$ instead of $-4$: $12 + b = 17$ gives $b = 5$. The value $17$ mistakes $f(3)$ for $f(0)$, ignoring that the function changes between those inputs."}
{"id": "SATM2U6VJ16", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A plumber charges a service call fee plus a constant rate per minute of work. The total charge in dollars for a job lasting $m$ minutes is modeled by $C(m) = 2.75m + 55$. Which statement best interprets the number $2.75$ in this context?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The plumber charges $\\$2.75$ per minute of work."}, {"key": "B", "text": "The plumber charges a flat fee of $\\$2.75$ for each service call."}, {"key": "C", "text": "Each additional minute of work reduces the total charge by $\\$2.75$."}, {"key": "D", "text": "The total charge for a 1-minute job is $\\$2.75$."}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "In the linear function $C(m) = 2.75m + 55$, the coefficient $2.75$ is the rate of change, representing the additional cost for each minute of work. Interpreting $2.75$ as a flat fee confuses the slope with the $y$-intercept; the flat fee is $\\$55$. The claim that each minute reduces the charge contradicts the positive slope. The total charge for a 1-minute job is $C(1) = 2.75 + 55 = 57.75$, not $\\$2.75$."}
{"id": "SATM2U9FY6Z", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "If $f(x) = 9x - 23$, what is the value of $f(4)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-13$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$13$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$36$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$59$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 4$ gives $f(4) = 9(4) - 23 = 36 - 23 = 13$. The value $-13$ results from a sign error, computing $23 - 36$ instead of $36 - 23$. The value $36$ comes from computing $9 \\times 4$ but stopping before subtracting $23$. The value $59$ comes from adding $23$ instead of subtracting it: $36 + 23 = 59$."}
{"id": "SATM2UU6NFJ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "If $f(x) = 11x - 37$, what is the value of $f(6)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-29$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$23$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$29$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$103$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is $29$. Substituting $x = 6$ into the function gives $f(6) = 11(6) - 37 = 66 - 37 = 29$. The value $-29$ results from reversing the subtraction order, computing $37 - 66$ instead of $66 - 37$. The value $23$ comes from an arithmetic slip in the multiplication step, computing $11 \\times 6 = 60$ instead of $66$, leading to $60 - 37 = 23$. The value $103$ results from adding $37$ instead of subtracting it, computing $66 + 37 = 103$."}
{"id": "SATM2V539CJ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -3x + 22$. A biologist uses this model to predict the number of bacteria colonies remaining in a sample $x$ hours after treatment begins. According to this model, by how many colonies does the count decrease each hour?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$22$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-3$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{22}{3}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $3$. In the function $g(x) = -3x + 22$, the slope is $-3$, which means the colony count decreases by $3$ each hour. The question asks for the amount of decrease, which is the absolute value of the slope. The value $22$ confuses the initial count (the $y$-intercept) with the rate of change. The value $-3$ gives the slope directly but does not correctly answer how many colonies decrease, since a decrease amount is expressed as a positive quantity. The value $\\frac{22}{3}$ results from dividing the $y$-intercept by the magnitude of the slope, which would give the $x$-intercept rather than the rate."}
{"id": "SATM2VBQOFU", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A function is defined by $f(x) = 3x - 4$. What is the value of $f(2)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-2$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$2$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$10$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$6$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is $2$. Substituting $x = 2$ into $f(x) = 3x - 4$ gives $f(2) = 3(2) - 4 = 6 - 4 = 2$. The value $-2$ results from a sign error, such as computing $4 - 6$ instead of $6 - 4$. The value $10$ comes from adding $4$ instead of subtracting, giving $6 + 4 = 10$. The value $6$ is the intermediate product $3(2) = 6$ before subtracting $4$."}
{"id": "SATM2VH7HBL", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "For the linear function $f$, $f(3) = 7$ and $f(7) = 19$. What is the rate of change of $f$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$3$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$12$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{1}{3}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$4$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $3$. The rate of change of a linear function equals $\\frac{f(7) - f(3)}{7 - 3} = \\frac{19 - 7}{7 - 3} = \\frac{12}{4} = 3$. $12$ is the numerator of the rate-of-change calculation, computed correctly but never divided by the change in $x$. $\\frac{1}{3}$ results from a conceptual gap: inverting the ratio to $\\frac{4}{12} = \\frac{1}{3}$. $4$ results from an arithmetic slip that divides the correct numerator, 12, by the wrong denominator, 3, instead of 4."}
{"id": "SATM2VNACQP", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A print shop charges a \\$67 setup fee plus \\$0.23 per flyer printed. If $C(n)$ represents the total cost, in dollars, for printing $n$ flyers, which of the following is the best interpretation of the number $0.23$ in the function $C(n) = 0.23n + 67$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The total cost increases by \\$0.23 for each additional flyer printed."}, {"key": "B", "text": "The total cost of printing 23 flyers."}, {"key": "C", "text": "The setup fee charged by the print shop is \\$0.23."}, {"key": "D", "text": "The minimum number of flyers that can be ordered."}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "In the function $C(n) = 0.23n + 67$, the coefficient $0.23$ is the rate of change, representing the amount by which the total cost increases for each additional flyer printed. Interpreting $0.23$ as the total cost of printing $23$ flyers confuses the coefficient with an input value and misreads the question. Interpreting $0.23$ as the setup fee confuses the slope with the constant term $67$, showing a concept gap. Interpreting $0.23$ as a minimum order quantity misidentifies a cost rate as a quantity constraint."}
{"id": "SATM2VUYE6N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A gym membership costs a one-time enrollment fee plus a fixed monthly rate. After 3 months of membership, the total amount paid is $\\$259$. After 8 months of membership, the total amount paid is $\\$479$. What is the monthly rate, in dollars?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\$44$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\$48$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\$55$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\$127$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Let $m$ be the monthly rate and $b$ the enrollment fee. Then $b + 3m = 259$ and $b + 8m = 479$. Subtracting the first equation from the second gives $5m = 220$, so $m = 44$. The monthly rate is $\\$44$. The value $\\$48$ results from an arithmetic error when dividing $220$ by $5$. The value $\\$55$ comes from incorrectly setting up the subtraction as $8m - 3m = 479 - 259 \\div 4$. The value $\\$127$ confuses the enrollment fee ($259 - 3(44) = 127$) with the monthly rate."}
{"id": "SATM2VZNFKC", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $g$ satisfies $g(3) = 19$ and $g(11) = -13$. For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "31/4", "explanation": "The slope is $m = \\frac{-13 - 19}{11 - 3} = \\frac{-32}{8} = -4$. Using the point $(3, 19)$: $g(x) = -4(x - 3) + 19 = -4x + 12 + 19 = -4x + 31$. Setting $g(x) = 0$: $-4x + 31 = 0$, so $4x = 31$, giving $x = \\frac{31}{4} = 7.75$. Verification: $g(7.75) = -4(7.75) + 31 = -31 + 31 = 0$."}
{"id": "SATM2W2NHLN", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A function is defined by $f(x) = 7x - 23$. What is the value of $f(5)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-12$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$2$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$12$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$58$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is $12$. Substitute $x = 5$: $f(5) = 7(5) - 23 = 35 - 23 = 12$. The value $-12$ results from a sign error, computing $23 - 35$ instead of $35 - 23$. The value $2$ comes from an equation setup error such as using $5 \\cdot 5 - 23 = 2$. The value $58$ results from adding $23$ to $35$ instead of subtracting."}
{"id": "SATM2WAL43R", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $g$ satisfies $g(3) = 19$ and $g(7) = 47$. What is the rate of change of $g$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-7$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{1}{7}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$28$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The rate of change of a linear function is the slope, computed as $\\frac{g(7) - g(3)}{7 - 3} = \\frac{47 - 19}{4} = \\frac{28}{4} = 7$. The value $-7$ results from reversing the order of subtraction in either the numerator or denominator but not both. The value $\\frac{1}{7}$ comes from inverting the slope formula, placing the change in input over the change in output. The value $28$ results from computing the change in output without dividing by the change in input."}
{"id": "SATM2WCICVM", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A water tank is being drained at a constant rate. At $t = 7$ minutes, the tank contains $206$ liters, and at $t = 23$ minutes, it contains $78$ liters. At what time, in minutes, will the tank be completely empty?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "131/4", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{78 - 206}{23 - 7} = \\frac{-128}{16} = -8$ liters per minute. Using the point $(7, 206)$, the volume function is $V(t) = -8(t - 7) + 206 = -8t + 56 + 206 = -8t + 262$. Setting $V(t) = 0$: $8t = 262$, so $t = \\frac{262}{8} = \\frac{131}{4}$. The tank will be completely empty at $t = \\frac{131}{4}$ minutes, or equivalently $32.75$ minutes."}
{"id": "SATM2WECJ3G", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(0) = 4$ and $f(2) = 10$. What is the value of $f(5)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$19$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$15$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$22$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$10$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is $19$. The rate of change is $\\frac{10 - 4}{2 - 0} = 3$, so $f(x) = 3x + 4$. Then $f(5) = 3(5) + 4 = 19$. $15$ results from computing only $3 \\times 5 = 15$ and forgetting to add the initial value of 4. $22$ results from an arithmetic slip when extending from $f(2) = 10$ by the wrong number of steps. $10$ restates $f(2)$, mistakenly answering for the wrong input value."}
{"id": "SATM2WGHYP0", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A plumber charges a flat fee of $\\$47$ plus $\\$29$ per hour for each service call. The total cost, in dollars, for a service call lasting $h$ hours is given by $C(h) = 29h + 47$. What does the value $29$ represent in this function?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The total cost of a one-hour service call"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The rate of change in cost per additional hour worked"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The fixed cost before any hours are worked"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The minimum number of hours for a service call"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "In the function $C(h) = 29h + 47$, the coefficient $29$ multiplies the variable $h$, making it the slope of the linear function. The slope represents the rate of change \u2014 the cost increases by $\\$29$ for each additional hour worked. The total cost of a one-hour call would be $C(1) = 29 + 47 = 76$, not $29$ alone. The fixed cost before any hours are worked is the constant term $47$, not $29$. Nothing in the function defines a minimum number of hours."}
{"id": "SATM2WGLKYW", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A plumber charges a fixed service fee plus a constant rate per hour of work. For a job lasting $4$ hours, the total charge is 218 dollars. For a job lasting $7$ hours, the total charge is 329 dollars. What is the plumber's fixed service fee, in dollars?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$37$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$47$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$70$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$148$"}], "correct_answer": "C", "explanation": "Let the fixed fee be $b$ and the hourly rate be $m$. Then $4m + b = 218$ and $7m + b = 329$. Subtracting the first equation from the second: $3m = 111$, so $m = 37$. Substituting back: $b = 218 - 4(37) = 218 - 148 = 70$. The fixed service fee is 70 dollars. The value $37$ is the hourly rate, not the fixed fee, reflecting partial reasoning that stops at the rate. The value $47$ comes from dividing the total charge of 329 dollars by 7 hours, ignoring the fixed fee entirely, an equation setup error. The value $148$ is $4 \\times 37$, the variable portion of the 4-hour charge, resulting from an arithmetic slip of reporting the product rather than subtracting it from the total."}
{"id": "SATM2WIRXFX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A swimming pool is being drained at a constant rate. At $t = 0$ hours, the pool contains 4,700 gallons of water. After 3 hours, it contains 2,540 gallons. Which function $g(t)$ models the gallons of water remaining after $t$ hours?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$g(t) = 4700 - 720t$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$g(t) = 4700 + 720t$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$g(t) = 2540 - 720t$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$g(t) = 4700 - 847t$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{2540 - 4700}{3} = \\frac{-2160}{3} = -720$ gallons per hour. With initial value 4,700, the function is $g(t) = 4700 - 720t$. The function $g(t) = 4700 + 720t$ uses a positive rate, implying the pool is filling. The function $g(t) = 2540 - 720t$ incorrectly uses the 3-hour value as the initial amount. The function $g(t) = 4700 - 847t$ results from dividing 2540 by 3 instead of computing the difference first."}
{"id": "SATM2WM7Z3A", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = 13x - 41$. What is the value of $f(5)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$18$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$24$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$26$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$106$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 5$ into $f(x) = 13x - 41$ gives $f(5) = 13(5) - 41 = 65 - 41 = 24$. The value is $24$."}
{"id": "SATM2WQ2RGT", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A plumber charges a flat service fee plus an hourly rate for each job. For a job lasting 2 hours, the total charge is 189 dollars. For a job lasting 5 hours, the total charge is 339 dollars. What is the plumber's hourly rate, in dollars?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$30$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$50$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$75$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$89$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Let the flat fee be $b$ and the hourly rate be $r$. Then $b + 2r = 189$ and $b + 5r = 339$. Subtracting the first equation from the second gives $3r = 150$, so $r = 50$. The hourly rate is 50 dollars. The value 30 comes from dividing the total difference of 150 by 5 (the hours from the larger job) instead of by 3 (the difference in hours), reflecting a misunderstanding of how to isolate the rate. The value 75 results from dividing 150 by 2 (the hours from the smaller job) instead of by the difference in hours, an error in setting up the elimination step. The value 89 is the flat fee ($b = 189 - 100 = 89$), which would be found by a student who correctly computes the rate but then reports the wrong quantity."}
{"id": "SATM2WT6TLN", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A water tank contains $185$ gallons and is being drained at a constant rate. After $7$ hours, the tank contains $87$ gallons. What is the rate of change, in gallons per hour, of the amount of water in the tank?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-14$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$14$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{87}{7}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-98$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{87 - 185}{7 - 0} = \\frac{-98}{7} = -14$ gallons per hour. The value $14$ has the correct magnitude but drops the negative sign, ignoring that the tank is draining. The value $-\\frac{87}{7}$ uses only the final amount divided by time rather than computing the change in water level. The value $-98$ gives the total change in gallons but does not divide by the elapsed time to find the hourly rate."}
{"id": "SATM2X3IAXQ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = 11x - 37$. What is the value of $g(6)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$29$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$103$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-31$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$66$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 6$: $g(6) = 11(6) - 37 = 66 - 37 = 29$. The value $103$ results from adding $37$ instead of subtracting it. The value $-31$ results from computing $6 - 37 = -31$ and ignoring the coefficient $11$. The value $66$ results from only computing $11 \\times 6$ without subtracting $37$."}
{"id": "SATM2X4MUD3", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -4x + 23$. What is the value of $f(7)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$51$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-5$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-51$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 7$: $f(7) = -4(7) + 23 = -28 + 23 = -5$. The value $51$ results from ignoring the negative sign on the coefficient, computing $4(7) + 23 = 51$. The value $5$ comes from computing the correct magnitude but dropping the negative sign. The value $-51$ results from computing $-(4 \\cdot 7 + 23) = -51$, incorrectly distributing the negative sign to both terms."}
{"id": "SATM2X7UG6N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -\\frac{4}{7}x + b$, where $b$ is a constant. The equation $y = g(x)$ is satisfied by the point $(14, -3)$. If $g(k) = 13$, what is the value of $k$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-28$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-14$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$14$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-\\frac{91}{4}$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "First find $b$ using the point $(14, -3)$: $-3 = -\\frac{4}{7}(14) + b = -8 + b$, so $b = 5$. Now solve $g(k) = 13$: $-\\frac{4}{7}k + 5 = 13$, giving $-\\frac{4}{7}k = 8$ and $k = 8 \\times (-\\frac{7}{4}) = -14$. The value $-28$ results from using $b = -3$ directly instead of solving for $b$, leading to $-\\frac{4}{7}k - 3 = 13$ and $k = -28$. The value $14$ comes from a sign error, using a positive slope $\\frac{4}{7}$ instead of $-\\frac{4}{7}$. The value $-\\frac{91}{4}$ results from omitting the step of finding $b$ and solving $-\\frac{4}{7}k = 13$ directly."}
{"id": "SATM2XN74HN", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -7x + 89$. For what value of $x$ does $f(x) = 19$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "10", "explanation": "Setting $f(x) = 19$ gives $-7x + 89 = 19$. Subtracting $89$ from both sides yields $-7x = -70$. Dividing both sides by $-7$ gives $x = 10$."}
{"id": "SATM2XWDKYD", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $h$ satisfies $h(3) = 32$ and $h(11) = -24$. For what value of $x$ does $h(x) = 0$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{71}{10}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{31}{4}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{83}{14}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{53}{7}$"}], "correct_answer": "D", "explanation": "The slope of $h$ is $\\frac{-24 - 32}{11 - 3} = \\frac{-56}{8} = -7$. Using point-slope form with $(3, 32)$: $h(x) = -7(x - 3) + 32 = -7x + 21 + 32 = -7x + 53$. Setting $h(x) = 0$: $-7x + 53 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{53}{7}$. The value $\\frac{71}{10}$ results from an arithmetic error when computing the intercept. The value $\\frac{31}{4}$ arises from incorrectly setting up the slope formula with the wrong denominator. The value $\\frac{83}{14}$ comes from using $\\frac{-56}{14}$ for the slope (doubling the denominator) and then solving with that incorrect slope."}
{"id": "SATM2XX02GZ", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $g$ satisfies $g(4) = -5$ and $g(-2) = 19$. For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 0$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "11/4", "explanation": "The slope of $g$ is $\\frac{19 - (-5)}{-2 - 4} = \\frac{24}{-6} = -4$. Using the point $(4, -5)$: $g(x) = -4(x - 4) + (-5) = -4x + 16 - 5 = -4x + 11$. Setting $g(x) = 0$ gives $-4x + 11 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{11}{4}$. A common error is computing the slope as $\\frac{24}{6} = 4$ by dropping the negative sign, which leads to the wrong zero. Another error is finding the correct function but solving $g(x) = 0$ incorrectly by dividing $11$ by $4$ with a sign mistake, yielding $-\\frac{11}{4}$."}
{"id": "SATM2XXO9AH", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "If $f(x) = 9x - 17$, what is the value of $f(7)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "46", "explanation": "Substitute $x = 7$ into the function: $f(7) = 9(7) - 17 = 63 - 17 = 46$."}
{"id": "SATM2Y6NAGX", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A packaging company charges a flat rate plus a per-item fee for shipping orders. Packaging 80 items costs 59 dollars, and packaging 200 items costs 107 dollars. Which function $f(n)$ gives the total cost, in dollars, of packaging $n$ items?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$f(n) = 0.4n + 27$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$f(n) = 0.4n + 59$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$f(n) = 2.5n + 27$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$f(n) = 0.4n - 27$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The per-item rate is $\\frac{107 - 59}{200 - 80} = \\frac{48}{120} = 0.4$ dollars per item. The flat rate is $59 - 0.4(80) = 59 - 32 = 27$ dollars. So $f(n) = 0.4n + 27$. The expression $0.4n + 59$ results from using the total cost for 80 items as the flat rate instead of computing the actual fixed charge. The expression $2.5n + 27$ results from inverting the rate calculation, dividing the change in items by the change in cost ($\\frac{120}{48} = 2.5$) instead of cost by items. The expression $0.4n - 27$ results from a sign error, subtracting the flat rate instead of adding it."}
{"id": "SATM2Y9OZ6V", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A linear function $g$ satisfies $g(2) = 17$ and $g(6) = 41$. What is the rate of change of $g$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$4$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$8$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$12$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{g(6) - g(2)}{6 - 2} = \\frac{41 - 17}{4} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6$. The value 4 comes from using the difference in $x$-values alone, misreading what the question asks. The value 8 results from an equation setup error such as dividing the difference in outputs by 3 instead of 4. The value 12 arises from an arithmetic slip, for instance computing $41 - 17 = 24$ and then halving instead of dividing by 4."}
{"id": "SATM2YANYDS", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "For the linear function $f$, $f(2) = -5$ and $f(6) = 19$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(x + 3) - 4$. What is $g(0)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$1$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-3$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-21$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$3$"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope of $f$ is $\\frac{19 - (-5)}{6 - 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6$. Using the point $(2, -5)$: $f(x) = 6x - 17$. Then $g(0) = f(0 + 3) - 4 = f(3) - 4 = (6 \\cdot 3 - 17) - 4 = 1 - 4 = -3$. The value $1$ comes from computing $f(3) = 1$ but forgetting to subtract $4$. The value $-21$ results from evaluating $f(0) - 4 = -17 - 4 = -21$, using $f(0)$ instead of $f(0 + 3)$. The value $3$ is a sign error on the correct answer."}
{"id": "SATM2YF00YP", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "A plumber charges a \\$65 service fee plus \\$42 per hour of labor. The total charge, in dollars, for $h$ hours of labor is modeled by the function $C(h) = 42h + 65$. What is the best interpretation of the number $42$ in this function?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "The total charge, in dollars, for each job"}, {"key": "B", "text": "The estimated increase in total charge, in dollars, for each additional hour of labor"}, {"key": "C", "text": "The initial charge, in dollars, before any labor is performed"}, {"key": "D", "text": "The maximum number of hours the plumber will work"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "In the function $C(h) = 42h + 65$, the coefficient $42$ is the rate of change, representing the increase in total charge for each additional hour of labor. The interpretation that $42$ is the total charge for each job ignores the service fee and the variable nature of the cost. The interpretation that $42$ is the initial charge confuses the slope with the $y$-intercept, which is $65$. The interpretation that $42$ is the maximum hours confuses a dollar rate with a time quantity."}
{"id": "SATM2YSU931", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "A linear function increases by 7 for every increase of 1 in $x$, and $f(0) = -11$. What is $f(3)$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "10", "explanation": "Since $f$ is linear with slope 7 and $y$-intercept $-11$, the function is $f(x) = 7x - 11$. Evaluating at $x = 3$ gives $f(3) = 7(3) - 11 = 21 - 11 = 10$."}
{"id": "SATM2Z0COKB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A researcher records the temperature of a chemical solution every minute. At $t = 0$ minutes, the temperature is 89 degrees Fahrenheit. The temperature decreases at a constant rate and reaches 47 degrees Fahrenheit at $t = 14$ minutes. At what time, in minutes, will the temperature first reach 26 degrees Fahrenheit?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$21$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$14$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$7$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$63$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The rate of change is $\\frac{47 - 89}{14 - 0} = \\frac{-42}{14} = -3$ degrees per minute. The linear function is $f(t) = -3t + 89$. Setting $f(t) = 26$: $-3t + 89 = 26$, so $-3t = -63$ and $t = 21$. The value $14$ is the time when the temperature reaches 47 degrees, not 26 degrees. The value $7$ results from computing the additional time past $t = 14$ for the temperature to drop from 47 to 26 degrees but neglecting to add 14 to get the total time. The value $63$ comes from computing $89 - 26 = 63$ without dividing by the rate of 3 degrees per minute."}
{"id": "SATM2Z8HC3N", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $h$ satisfies $h(4) = 23$ and $h(11) = -12$. For what value of $x$ does $h(x) = 0$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{43}{5}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-\\frac{3}{5}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$\\frac{23}{5}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$\\frac{12}{5}$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-12 - 23}{11 - 4} = \\frac{-35}{7} = -5$. Using point-slope form with the point $(4, 23)$: $h(x) = -5(x - 4) + 23 = -5x + 20 + 23 = -5x + 43$. Setting $h(x) = 0$ gives $-5x + 43 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{43}{5}$. The value $-\\frac{3}{5}$ results from using a slope of $+5$ (sign error), yielding $h(x) = 5x + 3$ and $x = -\\frac{3}{5}$. The value $\\frac{23}{5}$ comes from dividing $h(4) = 23$ by the slope magnitude without constructing the full equation. The value $\\frac{12}{5}$ comes from dividing $|h(11)| = 12$ by the slope magnitude."}
{"id": "SATM2ZE7NU9", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "A linear function $f$ satisfies $f(2) = 13$ and $f(8) = -11$. What is the value of $x$ when $f(x) = 0$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$\\frac{21}{4}$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$\\frac{13}{4}$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-\\frac{21}{4}$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$5$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "The slope is $\\frac{-11 - 13}{8 - 2} = \\frac{-24}{6} = -4$. Using point $(2, 13)$: $f(x) = -4(x - 2) + 13 = -4x + 8 + 13 = -4x + 21$. Setting $f(x) = 0$: $-4x + 21 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{21}{4}$. The value $\\frac{13}{4}$ results from dividing $f(2) = 13$ by the absolute slope 4 without accounting for the full linear equation. The value $-\\frac{21}{4}$ results from a sign error when solving $-4x + 21 = 0$. The value 5 comes from an arithmetic slip such as rounding $\\frac{21}{4}$ or miscalculating the $y$-intercept."}
{"id": "SATM2ZG8THY", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 1, "stem": "The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = -3x + 17$. What is the value of $g(4)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$29$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-29$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$14$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Substituting $x = 4$: $g(4) = -3(4) + 17 = -12 + 17 = 5$. The distractor $29$ results from treating the coefficient as positive, computing $3(4) + 17 = 29$ instead of $-3(4) + 17$. The distractor $-29$ results from an arithmetic slip where both terms are made negative: $-3(4) - 17 = -29$. The distractor $14$ comes from partial reasoning, computing $17 - 3 = 14$ without multiplying the coefficient by the input."}
{"id": "SATM2ZI6TUB", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $g$ is linear. If $g(3) = 17$ and $g(11) = 73$, what is the rate of change of $g$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "7", "explanation": "The rate of change of a linear function is its slope. Using the two given points: $\\frac{g(11) - g(3)}{11 - 3} = \\frac{73 - 17}{8} = \\frac{56}{8} = 7$."}
{"id": "SATM2ZKCLZ6", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The function $g$ is a linear function. The table below shows selected values of $g$.\r\n\r\n| $x$ | $g(x)$ |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| $-2$ | $17$ |\r\n| $3$ | $2$ |\r\n| $11$ | $-22$ |\r\n\r\nFor what value of $x$ does $g(x) = -7$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$6$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$8$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$5$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-6$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Using the first two points, the slope is $\\frac{2 - 17}{3 - (-2)} = \\frac{-15}{5} = -3$. The equation is $g(x) = -3(x - 3) + 2 = -3x + 9 + 2 = -3x + 11$. Setting $g(x) = -7$: $-3x + 11 = -7$, so $-3x = -18$ and $x = 6$. Verification: $g(6) = -3(6) + 11 = -7$. The distractor $8$ results from an arithmetic error such as computing $-3x = -24$ by incorrectly subtracting $11$ from both sides as $-7 - 11 = -24$ instead of $-7 - 11 = -18$. The distractor $5$ comes from a guess-and-check approach where $g(5) = -4$, which is not $-7$. The distractor $-6$ results from a sign error when dividing $-18$ by $-3$."}
{"id": "SATM2ZN0S79", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function $g$ is defined for all real numbers. The table below shows selected values of $g$.\r\n\r\n| $x$ | $g(x)$ |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| $-4$ | $31$ |\r\n| $2$ | $7$ |\r\n| $5$ | $-5$ |\r\n\r\nWhat is the value of $g(13)$?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "$-37$"}, {"key": "B", "text": "$-27$"}, {"key": "C", "text": "$-41$"}, {"key": "D", "text": "$-33$"}], "correct_answer": "A", "explanation": "Find the slope using any two points. Using $(2, 7)$ and $(5, -5)$: $m = \\frac{-5 - 7}{5 - 2} = \\frac{-12}{3} = -4$. Using point $(2, 7)$: $g(x) = -4(x - 2) + 7 = -4x + 8 + 7 = -4x + 15$. Then $g(13) = -4(13) + 15 = -52 + 15 = -37$. The value $-27$ results from computing $-4(13) + 15$ as $-42 + 15$ due to a multiplication slip ($4 \\times 13 = 42$ instead of $52$). The value $-41$ results from using slope $-4$ but writing the equation as $g(x) = -4x + 11$ by subtracting instead of adding the constant adjustment (sign error in the $y$-intercept). The value $-33$ results from computing the slope as $\\frac{-5 - 7}{5 - 2} = -4$ correctly but then using the change from $x = 5$ to $x = 13$ as $8$ steps of $-4$ and subtracting from $-5$: $-5 + (-4)(8) = -5 - 28 = -33$, an error of computing $4 \\times 8 = 28$ instead of $32$ (partial reasoning with arithmetic slip)."}
{"id": "SATM2ZT0BEM", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 2, "stem": "The function $f$ is defined by $f(x) = -\\frac{7}{3}x + 18$. For what value of $x$ does $f(x) = 4$?", "options": [], "correct_answer": "6", "explanation": "Set $f(x) = 4$: $-\\frac{7}{3}x + 18 = 4$. Subtract $18$ from both sides: $-\\frac{7}{3}x = -14$. Multiply both sides by $-\\frac{3}{7}$: $x = -14 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{7}) = \\frac{42}{7} = 6$."}
{"id": "SATM2ZXTQ48", "skill_codes": ["Linear Functions"], "difficulty": 3, "stem": "The linear function \\(g\\) is defined such that \\(g(1) = 23\\) and \\(g(9) = -17\\). For what value of \\(x\\) does \\(g(x) = 0\\)?", "options": [{"key": "A", "text": "\\(\\dfrac{33}{5}\\)"}, {"key": "B", "text": "\\(\\dfrac{28}{5}\\)"}, {"key": "C", "text": "\\(\\dfrac{23}{5}\\)"}, {"key": "D", "text": "\\(\\dfrac{18}{5}\\)"}], "correct_answer": "B", "explanation": "The slope of \\(g\\) is \\(\\frac{-17 - 23}{9 - 1} = \\frac{-40}{8} = -5\\). Using point-slope form with \\((1, 23)\\): \\(g(x) = -5(x - 1) + 23 = -5x + 5 + 23 = -5x + 28\\). Setting \\(g(x) = 0\\): \\(-5x + 28 = 0\\), so \\(x = \\frac{28}{5}\\). The value \\(\\frac{33}{5}\\) results from a sign error when computing the constant term. The value \\(\\frac{23}{5}\\) comes from using only the \\(y\\)-value at \\(x = 1\\) without adjusting for the slope offset. The value \\(\\frac{18}{5}\\) results from an arithmetic slip when adding 5 and 23."}
